% Origin: Kowalski, *Computational Logic and Human Thinking*,
%         Appendix A1 "The Syntax of Logic", section A1.4 ("Conditionals").
% Last verification: no open issues (no errors/warnings; all tests pass).
%
% The book clauses are:
%   amazing(X) <- can-fly(X) and isa(X, animal).
%   isa(X, animal) <- isa(X, person).
% and the English "any animal that can fly is amazing."
% In LE the taxonomy (isa) is expressed in the ontology, so the single rule
% "an animal is amazing if the animal can fly" carries the typed restriction:
% the head/body type "animal" only admits values that are animals (directly,
% e.g. a bird, or through the taxonomy, e.g. a person).

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
*an animal* is amazing.
*an animal* can fly.

the ontology is:
a bird is an animal.
a person is an animal.

the knowledge base amazing animals includes:

% any animal that can fly is amazing.
an animal is amazing
    if the animal can fly.

scenario story is:
    tweety can fly.
    tweety is a bird.
    clark can fly.
    clark is a person.
    waddles is a bird.
    amazers expects answers ["tweety is amazing", "clark is amazing"].

query amazers is:
    which animal is amazing.
